(*
ShipsBellNodeC6 — the C6 ship's bell as an always-on node.

Design: docs/design/2026-08-03-c6-shipsbell-always-on-node-design.md (cited D1-D10),
extending zephyr/boards/shipsBells/2026-07-26-shipsbellsv3-design.md (cited by §).

The rig could ring (§18) and could keep time (§16, §17.1) and could not do both
without a person typing: `ThreadNtpNode` syncs, prints and RETURNS, and the only
strike path in that image is `strike_console`, which needs a UART — so a
wall-powered board with no data cable had no trigger at all (§18.5). This node is
the smallest thing that is a bell: it never returns, it reads the DS3231, and it
strikes the watch on the half hour.

  D1  The trigger is a 1 s POLL of the RTC, posted on `clock/tick`. Alarm2 (§16.4)
      is deferred on this device, not scoped away — the INT/SQW line IS wired to
      GPIO10. Nothing above `clock/tick` knows what produced the tick, so swapping
      the poll for the ISR later is a change to one registration.
  D2  The slot is DERIVED, never counted: `slot := secondsOfDay // slotSeconds`.
      A missed tick, a late tick, a GC pause and a reboot all answer the same,
      because the answer is a property of the TIME and not of this node's history.
      `lastSlot` is a has-this-one-rung marker. It is not a clock.
  D3  The pattern lives here; C keeps only `gpioSet`. `strike_console` in
      src/main.c stays exactly where it is, as the bench instrument §11.2 called it.
      The COUNT is not re-implemented: `Bell>>bellsForHalfHour:` owns it (i mod 8,
      0 reading as 8; plain modulo, so NO dog watches — 18:30 rings 5).
  D4  A missed strike is skipped silently and the marker never moves backward.
      Power restored at 21:45 does not ring 21:30's three; the next sound is
      22:00's four. A bell at a time that is not a half hour is worse than a
      missing bell — the missing one is absent, the wrong one is misinformation.
  D5  Midnight is a CASE, not an inequality. `slot > lastSlot` is false at the
      47 -> 0 wrap. This exact wrap rang eight bells twice on 2026-07-24.
  D6  The clock may be improved by the network and never stopped by it: one sync
      at boot, then one immediately after the MIDNIGHT striking completes.
      `ntpSyncTz:` blocks up to the SNTP timeout, so hanging it off the end of the
      striking satisfies "only between strikes" by construction.
  D7  Never strike before the time is known. `wallClock` nil -> poll, print, do
      not ring. Silence is the correct failure mode for a bell; a guess is not.
  D8  `slotSeconds` is 1800 in the product and 60 on the bench: four boundaries in
      four minutes with the ratchet, the wrap, the phrasing and the in-flight
      guard all running unchanged. Unlike MagTagClockNode's deleted 6x demo tick,
      this does NOT advance the clock — only how often the derived index changes.

WIDTH IS 10 ms, NOT 50. The design record says 50; 50 is `BELL_MAX_WIDTH_MS`, the
pattern-path duty cap in src/main.c. What actually rang on 2026-08-03 and was
approved by ear was `bells: width 10 ms, pair gap 500 ms, set gap 1600 ms`
(collector log 21:19:01 through 21:21:12, b4/b8/b3). These constants are that line.
They are a piezo result: §18.3 says a continuous-tone element has no decay and
cannot settle a cadence for a gong — but §19 then took the solenoid off THIS device
for good, so here the piezo is the ringer and not a stand-in.

ONE `Timers` INSTANCE, held in a slot. `platform timers` answers a FRESH instance
per call, each with its own `pending` registry, and `dispatch:` silently drops a
token it does not know. Registering on one and dispatching on another cost
MagTagClockNode an entire overnight run: 0 of ~19 bells, no crash, no output.

The reactor drain is `pumpEvents` + `drain`, and `pumpEvents` BLOCKS (K_FOREVER)
on the pulse mailbox until the k_timer ISR posts — which is exactly right for a
node whose every input is a timer, and would be a hang for one that also polls
(TimerNode/SoakNode/PortaBridgeNodeC6 all take this shape).

BUILD IT WITH THE NTP ADDRESS OR THE DAILY SYNC IS DEAD ON ARRIVAL:

  OEVM_NTP_SERVER6=fdc5:9109:9afe:97ea:9541:d963:f8d9:8567 \
  ./run.sh --board esp32c6 --node ShipsBellNodeC6.nsl \
           --conf-extra prj_esp32c6_thread.conf --flash

`OEVM_SOLENOID_CONSOLE=1` is ABSENT on purpose here too, and for the same reason as
in `ShipsBellAlarmNodeC6.nsl` — read the note there (rete#182). It is not merely
unnecessary on this node, it is wrong twice over: §19 took the solenoid off this
device for good, so the ringer is the piezo and there is nothing for a console
striker to strike; and the expect line below says "USB carrying only power", which
is the undriven RX the design's rule is about.

Without `OEVM_NTP_SERVER6` main.c falls back to `sntp_simple("pool.ntp.org")`, and
the porta mesh is a ULA with no DNS64 and no `CONFIG_DNS_RESOLVER` — so it answers
`ntp: sntp_simple rc=-2` on every boot, forever, and the node correctly shrugs and
rings off the RTC. It is a build-time omission that looks exactly like a network
outage. Read the printk to tell them apart: `sntp_simple_addr(` means the address
was compiled in, bare `sntp_simple` means it was not. The env var is read at CMake
CONFIGURE time, so adding it needs `--pristine` (2026-08-04).

Dialect: `[ ... ] whileTrue:`, never `repeat`. No modulo in nsl — every one is
spelled `n - ((n // d) * d)`. State lives in instance slots.

expect (C6 console, wall-powered, USB carrying only power):
shipsbell ready, slot 1800 s, 10/500/1600 ms
bell slot 44 at 22:00:00 — 4 bells
*)
class ShipsBellNodeC6 packageUsing: manifest = (
  | plat space timers tick bellCh
    lastSlot. striking ::= false. strokeCount ::= 0. strokeIndex ::= 0. pinOn ::= false.
    haveClock ::= false. gpioMissing ::= false. midnightPending ::= false. ticks ::= 0. | ) (

  (* --- boot --------------------------------------------------------------- *)

  public main: platform args: args = (
    plat:: platform.
    space:: plat space.

    (* HOLD ONE. See the header — this is the single easiest thing to get wrong
       in this file, and its failure mode is silence. *)
    timers:: plat timers.
    space whenever: 'sys/timer' do: [ :e | timers dispatch: e ].

    tick:: space channel: 'clock/tick'.
    tick whenever: '' do: [ :m | self onTick ].

    bellCh:: space channel: 'bell/stroke'.
    bellCh whenever: '' do: [ :m | self strokeStep ].

    ('shipsbell ready, slot ' , self slotSeconds printString , ' s, ' ,
      self widthMillis printString , '/' , self pairGapMillis printString , '/' ,
      self setGapMillis printString , ' ms') out.

    (* D6/D7: one sync before anything is scheduled. It blocks up to the SNTP
       timeout, which is free here — no bell can be late yet, and a board that has
       been off may have been off long enough to matter. A nil is shrugged off and
       the RTC carries on. *)
    self syncClock.
    self showClock.

    (* D1. The FIRST tick seeds `lastSlot` and rings nothing (D4). *)
    timers every: self pollMillis post: #tick on: tick.

    [ true ] whileTrue: [ plat pumpEvents. space drain ].
  )

  (* --- the schedule ------------------------------------------------------- *)

  (* One poll. Everything here is re-derived from the clock; nothing accumulates. *)
  onTick = (
    | secs slot |
    ticks:: ticks + 1.
    secs:: plat wallClock.
    secs == nil ifTrue: [ ^ self noClock ].

    haveClock ifFalse: [
      haveClock:: true.
      ('clock ' , (self hms: secs) , ' — readings good') out ].

    slot:: secs // self slotSeconds.

    (* D4: the boot seed rings nothing. Restored at 21:45, the next sound is 22:00. *)
    lastSlot == nil ifTrue: [
      lastSlot:: slot.
      ('seed slot ' , slot printString , ' at ' , (self hms: secs) ,
        ' — nothing rung') out.
      ^ self ].

    (self advancedTo: slot from: lastSlot) ifFalse: [ ^ self heartbeat: secs ].

    lastSlot:: slot.
    self ringSlot: slot at: secs.
    ^ self )

  (* THE RATCHET, and the midnight case (D4, D5). Forward is a boundary; equal is
     the same half hour, already rung; backward is a clock correction and stays
     SILENT — unless it lands on slot 0, which is midnight however it was reached,
     and midnight is where the plain `slot > lastSlot` reading rang eight bells
     twice on 2026-07-24. *)
  advancedTo: slot from: prev = (
    slot = prev  ifTrue: [ ^ false ].
    slot > prev  ifTrue: [ ^ true ].
    ^ slot = 0 )

  (* D3: the COUNT comes from Bell, which owns the convention. This node owns only
     when a half hour happened and what to do about it. *)
  ringSlot: slot at: secs = (
    | n |
    n:: (plat bell) bellsForHalfHour: slot.
    ('bell slot ' , slot printString , ' at ' , (self hms: secs) , ' — ' ,
      n printString , (self bellWord: n)) out.
    slot = 0 ifTrue: [ midnightPending:: true ].    (* D6: sync AFTER the striking *)
    self startPattern: n.
    ^ self )

  (* D7. A nil mid-run is a dead part or a bus fault: stop striking, say so ONCE,
     and resume when readings resume. Silence is the correct failure mode. *)
  noClock = (
    haveClock ifTrue: [
      haveClock:: false.
      'clock nil — no DS3231 reading; not striking until it returns' out.
      ^ self ].
    (ticks = 1) ifTrue: [
      'clock nil at boot — polling, not striking (no DS3231, or never set)' out ].
    ^ self )

  (* Liveness in the collector without a line a second: 86400 of those a day would
     bury the ~48 that carry the product. *)
  heartbeat: secs = (
    (ticks - ((ticks // self printEveryTicks) * self printEveryTicks)) = 0
      ifTrue: [ ('tick ' , (self hms: secs) , ' slot ' , lastSlot printString) out ].
    ^ self )

  (* --- the striker: a state machine, so the poll keeps running -------------- *)

  (* `set: true` -> one-shot at WIDTH -> `set: false` -> one-shot at the pair or
     set gap -> next stroke. Odd stroke (1-based) = first of a pair, so the SHORT
     gap follows it and holds the pair together; even = the long gap separates the
     pairs; the last stroke gets no trailing gap, which is what produces the lone
     final stroke of an odd count. `b3` is the rung that proves both branches. *)
  startPattern: n = (
    striking ifTrue: [
      (* Unreachable at 1800 s, reachable at D8's 60 s. Two rings interleaved on
         one pin is neither ring. *)
      ('bells ' , n printString , ' REFUSED — a pattern is already in flight') out.
      ^ self ].
    ((n < 1) or: [ n > 8 ]) ifTrue: [
      ('bells ' , n printString , ' REFUSED (allowed 1..8)') out.
      ^ self ].
    strokeCount:: n.
    strokeIndex:: 0.
    striking:: true.
    ^ self strikeOn )

  strokeStep = (
    pinOn ifTrue: [ ^ self strikeOff ].
    ^ self strikeOn )

  strikeOn = (
    strokeIndex:: strokeIndex + 1.
    pinOn:: true.
    self pin: true.
    timers after: self widthMillis post: #stroke on: bellCh.
    ^ self )

  strikeOff = (
    pinOn:: false.
    self pin: false.
    strokeIndex = strokeCount ifTrue: [
      striking:: false.
      'bells done' out.
      ^ self afterPattern ].
    timers after: (self gapAfter: strokeIndex) post: #stroke on: bellCh.
    ^ self )

  (* No modulo in nsl. Odd index -> within a pair; even -> between pairs. *)
  gapAfter: i = (
    (i - ((i // 2) * 2)) = 1 ifTrue: [ ^ self pairGapMillis ].
    ^ self setGapMillis )

  (* D6: the daily sync is an EVENT — "after the midnight bells" — rather than a
     constant. That satisfies kernel.nsl's "call it only between strikes" by
     construction, instead of by a 300 s guess that has to be re-justified if eight
     bells ever take longer. *)
  afterPattern = (
    midnightPending ifTrue: [
      midnightPending:: false.
      self syncClock.
      self showClock ].
    ^ self )

  (* A pin the board never declared RAISES rather than answering a plausible false
     (tuvm#82). Caught here and said ONCE: a bell with no ringer should still keep
     time and still say why it is silent. *)
  pin: aBool = (
    [ (plat gpio) set: self strikePin to: aBool ]
      on: [ :e | e tag = #gpioUndeclared ]
      do: [ :e |
        gpioMissing ifFalse: [
          gpioMissing:: true.
          ('bell: GPIO' , self strikePin printString ,
            ' not declared on this board — keeping time, ringing nothing') out ] ].
    ^ self )

  (* --- the clock ---------------------------------------------------------- *)

  (* One sync. nil at any step leaves the RTC alone and the bell ringing off it
     (MagTagClockNode's rule) — the network may improve this clock and may never
     stop it. The date comes from the same response as the time: the RTC will not
     accept a time without one, and the bell itself never reads the date. *)
  syncClock = (
    | secs date rc |
    secs:: plat ntpSyncTz: self tzOffset.
    secs == nil ifTrue: [ 'ntp miss — keeping the RTC' out. ^ self ].
    date:: plat ntpDate.
    date == nil ifTrue: [ 'ntp date miss — keeping the RTC' out. ^ self ].
    rc:: plat wallClockSetDate: date time: secs.
    rc == nil ifTrue: [ 'rtc write REFUSED — keeping the RTC' out. ^ self ].
    ('ntp ok ' , date printString , ' ' , (self hms: secs)) out.
    ^ self )

  (* A write that answered 0 is not evidence the part holds the time. *)
  showClock = (
    | secs |
    secs:: plat wallClock.
    secs == nil
      ifTrue:  [ 'rtc nil (no DS3231, or never set)' out ]
      ifFalse: [ ('rtc ' , (self hms: secs) , '  slot ' ,
                   (secs // self slotSeconds) printString) out ].
    ^ self )

  (* seconds-since-midnight -> 'HH:MM:SS', zero-padded so a serial log lines up.
     No modulo: each field is a division and a subtraction. *)
  hms: secs = (
    | h rem m s |
    h::   secs // 3600.
    rem:: secs - (h * 3600).
    m::   rem // 60.
    s::   rem - (m * 60).
    ^ (self pad: h) , ':' , (self pad: m) , ':' , (self pad: s) )

  pad: n = ( ^ n < 10 ifTrue: [ '0' , n printString ] ifFalse: [ n printString ] )

  (* One bell is a bell. The log is read by a person. *)
  bellWord: n = ( ^ n = 1 ifTrue: [ ' bell' ] ifFalse: [ ' bells' ] )

  (* --- constants ---------------------------------------------------------- *)

  (* D8. 1800 in the product; 60 on the bench, which is a ONE-LINE edit and a
     rebuild. At 60 the counts stop matching wall time (1..8 cycling every eight
     minutes) and nothing else changes — eight bells is ~7 s, so a pattern still
     finishes well inside the slot. *)
  slotSeconds     = ( ^ 1800 )
  pollMillis      = ( ^ 1000 )

  (* §8/§15.1: GPIO18, ACTIVE_HIGH, de-energised at boot by wire_gpio_outputs().
     The node names the silkscreen number; main.c matches it against the `gpioout`
     alias. Do not invert the polarity — see the overlay. *)
  strikePin       = ( ^ 18 )

  (* The line that rang on 2026-08-03 and was approved by ear. See the header for
     why this is 10 and the design record says 50. *)
  widthMillis     = ( ^ 10 )
  pairGapMillis   = ( ^ 500 )
  setGapMillis    = ( ^ 1600 )

  (* One liveness line every 5 minutes, so a silent watch is distinguishable from
     a dead node without burying the log. *)
  printEveryTicks = ( ^ 300 )

  (* US/Arizona: UTC-7 and no DST, so a constant is honest here in a way it would
     not be anywhere that observes summer time. *)
  tzOffset        = ( ^ -25200 )
) : (
)
